('127.297.d.7.E',4,'A marketer filters a database to find customers who bought in the last month. This is a:','["Merge", "Slogan", "Chart", "Query"]'::jsonb,3,'Queries select matching records.','original'),
('127.297.d.7.F',3,'A spreadsheet can help a marketer to:','["Calculate totals and make charts", "Send faxes", "Edit videos", "Design logos only"]'::jsonb,0,'Spreadsheets calculate and chart.','original'),
('127.297.d.7.F',4,'To total sales in cells B2 through B10, a marketer could use:','["=TOTAL B", "=SUM(B2:B10)", "SUM B2", "=B2+B10 only"]'::jsonb,1,'SUM adds a range.','original'),
('127.297.d.8.A',3,'A working portfolio might include a resume, cover letter, and:','["Birthday cards", "Game scores", "Work samples", "Receipts from lunch"]'::jsonb,2,'Portfolios show skills.','original'),
('127.297.d.8.A',4,'A portfolio should be kept:','["Disorganized", "Hidden", "Blank", "Updated and organized"]'::jsonb,3,'Maintained portfolios are useful.','original'),
('127.297.d.8.B',3,'Employers may look at an applicant''s public social media to judge:','["Professionalism and judgment", "Hair length", "Shoe size", "Favorite color"]'::jsonb,0,'Online presence reflects character.','original'),
('127.297.d.8.B',4,'A student should remove posts that could harm employability, such as those that show:','["Awards", "Offensive or unprofessional behavior", "Volunteer work", "School achievements"]'::jsonb,1,'Clean up unprofessional content.','original'),
('127.297.d.8.C',3,'A common interview question is:','["''Where is my pen?''", "''What is the weather?''", "''Tell me about yourself.''", "''Can you juggle?''"]'::jsonb,2,'Interviewers ask about background.','original'),
('127.297.d.8.C',4,'Showing interview skills includes arriving early, dressing appropriately, and:','["Leaving early", "Interrupting", "Texting during questions", "Giving thoughtful answers with eye contact"]'::jsonb,3,'Professional behavior matters.','original'),
('127.297.d.9.A',3,'A trend affecting marketing today is:','["Increased use of mobile devices for shopping", "No data use", "Less technology", "Fewer ads online"]'::jsonb,0,'Mobile commerce is growing.','original'),
('127.297.d.9.A',4,'Customers who expect fast online delivery are driving a trend toward:','["Fewer products", "Faster, more flexible fulfillment", "Slower shipping", "No tracking"]'::jsonb,1,'Customer expectations shape trends.','original'),
('127.297.d.9.B',3,'Researching emerging technologies helps marketers to:','["Avoid customers", "Delete data", "Find new ways to reach customers", "Stop advertising"]'::jsonb,2,'New tools create new opportunities.','original'),
('127.297.d.9.B',4,'Which is an emerging marketing technology?','["Typewriters", "Phone books", "Carbon paper", "Artificial intelligence that personalizes offers"]'::jsonb,3,'AI personalizes marketing.','original'),
('127.297.d.10.A',3,'Diversity affects marketing because customers have different:','["Backgrounds, needs, and preferences", "Shoe sizes only", "Phone brands only", "Heights only"]'::jsonb,0,'Diverse customers want different things.','original'),
('127.297.d.10.A',4,'An ad that includes people of many backgrounds is likely to:','["Offend everyone", "Appeal to a wider range of customers", "Confuse sales", "Reduce reach"]'::jsonb,1,'Inclusion broadens appeal.','original'),
('127.297.d.10.B',3,'Multiculturalism in marketing means recognizing:','["Only one culture", "Only tourists", "Many cultures among customers", "No cultures"]'::jsonb,2,'Marketers respect cultural differences.','original'),
('127.297.d.10.B',4,'Multigenerational marketing considers that:','["Everyone is the same age", "Only youth matter", "Age never matters", "Different age groups use different media and have different values"]'::jsonb,3,'Generations differ.','original'),
('127.297.d.11.A',3,'An economic good is something that:','["Is tangible and can satisfy a want", "Cannot be seen or used", "Is free of cost always", "Is only a feeling"]'::jsonb,0,'Goods are physical items.','original'),
('127.297.d.11.A',4,'Which is a service rather than a good?','["A book", "A haircut", "A chair", "A phone"]'::jsonb,1,'Services are intangible activities.','original'),
('127.297.d.11.B',3,'A need is something that is:','["A fashion trend", "Always a luxury", "Necessary for survival", "A hobby"]'::jsonb,2,'Needs are essential.','original'),
('127.297.d.11.B',4,'Which is a want rather than a need?','["Shelter", "Food", "Water", "A designer watch"]'::jsonb,3,'Wants are not essential.','original'),
('127.297.d.11.C',3,'Utility is the:','["Usefulness or satisfaction a good or service provides", "Price of a product", "Color of a product", "Tax on a sale"]'::jsonb,0,'Utility measures value to the user.','original'),
('127.297.d.11.C',4,'A store open late adds which type of utility?','["Place utility", "Time utility", "Possession utility", "Form utility"]'::jsonb,1,'Convenient hours add time utility.','original'),
('127.297.d.11.D',3,'Prices in a market act as:','["Decorations", "Random numbers", "Signals that guide buyers and sellers", "Secrets"]'::jsonb,2,'Prices communicate scarcity and value.','original'),
('127.297.d.11.D',4,'A rising price for a scarce item tells producers to:','["Stop selling", "Close shops", "Hide stock", "Consider making more"]'::jsonb,3,'Higher prices encourage supply.','original'),
('127.297.d.11.E',3,'When demand rises and supply stays constant, the price usually:','["Increases", "Decreases", "Stays zero", "Disappears"]'::jsonb,0,'More demand pushes prices up.','original'),
('127.297.d.11.E',4,'When supply increases and demand stays constant, the price usually:','["Increases", "Decreases", "Doubles", "Stays the same"]'::jsonb,1,'More supply lowers prices.','original'),
('127.297.d.12.A',3,'The basic economic questions include what to produce, how to produce it, and:','["Why to laugh", "Where to sleep", "For whom to produce it", "When to nap"]'::jsonb,2,'What, how, for whom.','original'),
('127.297.d.12.A',4,'In a market economy, the question of what to produce is mostly decided by:','["A central planner only", "Tradition only", "Luck", "Consumers and producers through the market"]'::jsonb,3,'Markets guide production.','original'),
('127.297.d.12.B',3,'Most economies are mixed because they combine:','["Private enterprise and government involvement", "Only barter", "No businesses", "No government"]'::jsonb,0,'Mixed means both.','original'),
('127.297.d.12.B',4,'Public roads and private shops operating in the same country show a:','["Pure command economy", "Mixed economy", "Pure barter economy", "Traditional economy"]'::jsonb,1,'Public and private mix.','original'),
('127.297.d.12.C',3,'One way government affects business is by:','["Choosing company logos", "Planning lunch menus", "Setting regulations and collecting taxes", "Naming products"]'::jsonb,2,'Regulation and taxation.','original'),
('127.297.d.12.C',4,'Government may provide goods like roads that benefit businesses and:','["Only foreigners", "No one", "Only competitors", "Society as a whole"]'::jsonb,3,'Public goods help all.','original'),
('127.297.d.13.A',3,'A characteristic of private enterprise is:','["Private ownership of businesses", "Government owns everything", "No profit", "No competition at all"]'::jsonb,0,'Private ownership defines it.','original'),
('127.297.d.13.A',4,'In a private enterprise system, businesses decide:','["Only what government says", "What to sell and how to price it", "Nothing about quality", "Nothing"]'::jsonb,1,'Business freedom.','original'),
('127.297.d.13.B',3,'An advantage of private enterprise is:','["No risk", "No competition", "Incentive to innovate and earn profit", "Guaranteed success"]'::jsonb,2,'Profit motivates innovation.','original'),
('127.297.d.13.B',4,'A disadvantage of private enterprise can be:','["No freedom", "No choices", "No profits", "Unequal incomes and business failure"]'::jsonb,3,'Risk and inequality are drawbacks.','original'),
('127.297.d.13.C',3,'Two gas stations lowering prices to attract customers is an example of:','["Price competition", "Taxation", "Monopoly", "Barter"]'::jsonb,0,'Price competition lowers costs for buyers.','original'),
('127.297.d.13.C',4,'Competing on quality, service, or features rather than price is:','["Subsidy", "Nonprice competition", "Monopoly", "Price war"]'::jsonb,1,'Nonprice competition emphasizes features.','original'),
('127.297.d.14.A',3,'Which is an economic measurement?','["Shoe size", "Pet name", "Gross domestic product", "Phone color"]'::jsonb,2,'GDP measures output.','original'),
('127.297.d.14.B',3,'Businesses use economic measures to:','["Choose colors", "Pick holidays", "Name products", "Plan production and investment"]'::jsonb,3,'Measures guide planning.','original'),
('127.297.d.14.B',4,'Rising GDP may lead a business to:','["Expect higher customer spending", "Close stores", "Cut quality", "Expect no sales"]'::jsonb,0,'Growth boosts demand.','original'),
('127.297.d.14.C',3,'Price stability means that prices:','["Are unknown", "Do not change drastically over time", "Fall to zero", "Double each year"]'::jsonb,1,'Stable prices help planning.','original'),
('127.297.d.14.C',4,'Price stability helps businesses because it makes:','["Costs random", "Sales impossible", "Planning and budgeting more predictable", "Customers vanish"]'::jsonb,2,'Predictability helps decisions.','original'),
('127.297.d.14.D',3,'Consumer spending is important because it:','["Only affects taxes", "Never changes", "Has no effect", "Drives a large share of economic activity"]'::jsonb,3,'Spending fuels the economy.','original'),
('127.297.d.14.D',4,'If consumer spending drops sharply, businesses may:','["Reduce production and hiring", "Expand rapidly", "Ignore sales", "Raise hiring"]'::jsonb,0,'Lower demand reduces activity.','original'),
('127.297.d.14.E',3,'A high unemployment rate means:','["Everyone is working", "Many people who want jobs cannot find them", "Prices are zero", "Businesses are booming"]'::jsonb,1,'Unemployment signals weak job markets.','original'),
('127.297.d.14.E',4,'High unemployment can reduce business sales because:','["They stop eating", "Stores close for free", "Unemployed people have less money to spend", "They buy more"]'::jsonb,2,'Less income, less spending.','original'),
('127.297.d.14.F',3,'Inflation is a:','["Tax cut", "General fall in prices", "Stock tip", "General rise in prices over time"]'::jsonb,3,'Inflation raises price levels.','original'),
('127.297.d.14.F',4,'During inflation, a business'' costs for materials usually:','["Increase", "Fall", "Stay fixed forever", "Vanish"]'::jsonb,0,'Inflation raises costs.','original'),
('127.297.d.15.A',3,'A business cycle is the pattern of:','["Tides", "Expansion and contraction in the economy", "Seasons only", "Day and night"]'::jsonb,1,'Cycles rise and fall.','original'),
('127.297.d.15.A',4,'The four phases of the business cycle are expansion, peak, contraction, and:','["Valley of quiet only", "Plateau forever", "Trough", "Harvest"]'::jsonb,2,'The low point is the trough.','original'),
('127.297.d.15.B',3,'During an expansion, businesses typically:','["Fire everyone", "Close stores", "Stop selling", "Hire more and increase production"]'::jsonb,3,'Growth encourages hiring.','original'),
('127.297.d.15.B',4,'During a contraction, consumers often:','["Reduce spending", "Stop paying taxes", "Spend wildly", "Ignore prices"]'::jsonb,0,'Spending slows in downturns.','original'),
